# Lane A addendum — COPY STANDARDS (2026-09-09)

Rides with `prompts/lane-a-website-execute-2026-09-09.md`. Binding on every copy
change Lane A makes. Written after Jordan's 17:50 EDT ruling that the live gated
site reads **"immature"**. The three defects below were fixed and shipped in
`matchworkroofing` commit `6a658d4`; this addendum exists so they cannot come back.

## 1. American English only — machine-banned

The four-trade build shipped in British English (169 spellings in `src/`, plus
374 in the wider sweep). All of it is now a hard build failure. `build.py` is the
enforcer and `tools/verify.py` re-checks the built output; **the two lists must
stay in sync**. Banned regexes, case-folded:

`\bcolour` `\baluminium\b` `\bgrey` `\bmould` `\bneighbour` `\bcentre` `\bstorey`
`\blabour` `\borganis` `\bcatalogue` `\btravell` `\bprogramme` `\bkerb\b`
`\bitemis` `\bfibre`

Exempt by name, not by rule: **ColorPlus** (James Hardie product) is already US
spelling. Never "fix" a slug, `id`, class or URL into American spelling — a URL
change needs a redirect, and `_redirects` is inert on GitHub Pages. (Checked
2026-09-09: no slug or id carries a British spelling, so nothing is owed here.)
One real trap, learned the hard way: `aria-labelledby` is a **W3C attribute
name**, not prose. A blanket `labell→label` pass corrupts it on every page.

## 2. No crew-structure claims that are not literally true

Jordan, verbatim: *"Four trades, one crew / No subcontracted handoffs"* is
**"retarded — anyone who knows contracting knows it would be done by different
crews."** He is right, and it was on the hero of the home page.

What is literally true: **Matchwork sells, measures, schedules, supervises and
warrants the work. The trade crews differ by trade.** The allowed framing is
**one company / one contract / one project lead / one warranty / one clean site.**
Banned at build time: `\bone crew\b`, `\bsubcontracted handoff`.

The ban catches the phrase, not the lie. Nine more instances said the same false
thing in other words ("our own crew", "the crew that measures your eave is the
crew that puts the metal on it", "run through the same crew", "handled by the same
crew in the same visit"). **Read for the claim, not the string.**

## 3. No stale-state lines

`/contact/` still said *"Our phone number is still being set up"* eleven hours
after (614) 741-1393 shipped. Banned: `\bstill being set up\b`. The standard:
**every fact that can change is a `config.json` / FACTS token behind
`{{#if}}`, or it does not ship.** "Coming soon", "TBD" and "placeholder" in prose
are the same defect — the existing `{{#unless PHONE_E164}}TBD{{/unless}}` pattern
is the correct shape and is fine.

## 4. The "immature" standard

Jordan's word for copy that sounds like a brochure written by someone who has
never run a job. The fix is not softer adjectives. It is **specificity, proof,
trade-accurate sequencing, an adult tone, and no cute eyebrows.** If a working
contractor would laugh at a sentence, it fails — regardless of how it reads.

## 5. Lane A's FIRST copy task, before the typography ship

Read **all 52 pages** looking for claims a working contractor would laugh at.
Report to Jordan as a **numbered list with a proposed replacement for each**.
Do not ship the typography change until he has that list.

## 6. Every replacement is shown before/after

Never change a line of Jordan's site copy silently. Before/after, in chat, in his
own reading order. He caught both defects in this batch himself, from the live
site, and that is the failure this rule closes.
